---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: eprfs-local-verified-materialization
purpose: >
  Host-aware local projection and drift inspection over content-addressed bytes. This
  crate converts a verified tree or projection into ordinary files; the on-disk view is
  reconstructable and never becomes the authority for its source.
rules:
  - id: verify-fetched-bytes-before-writing
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.rs"
      contains-any: ["fetch_blob", "write_file", "write_symlink", "materialize_entry"]
    dedupe-of: "elohim/eprfs/eprfs-core/src/address.rs (BlobCid::verifies)"
    why: >
      In eprfs-local, check the returned handle CID and verify fetched bytes against
      the requested BlobCid before any destination write. BlobCid::verifies dispatches
      by the CID's declared raw or DAG-CBOR codec; do not hardcode one codec.
      Keep this check on every LocalOnly and FetchIfMissing file/symlink path.
      Whole-tree restore uses restore_exact_tree in exact_tree.rs; never infer its
      guarantees from the legacy entry-by-entry materializer.
  - id: preserve-host-file-semantics
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.rs"
      contains-any: ["SymlinkMode", "executable_bits", "apply_executable_bit", "atomic_rename"]
    dedupe-of: "elohim/eprfs/eprfs-host/src/lib.rs (HostProfile capabilities)"
    why: >
      Use eprfs-host's HostProfile when materializing mode and symlink semantics.
      Preserve symlink target bytes and executable mode when the host supports them;
      refuse an exact restore when it cannot express required semantics. A marker or
      sparse projection must not be reported as an exact restored tree. Stage and
      promote verified content safely without overwriting an existing destination
      before verification succeeds. Git mode/index semantics belong in brit.
---

# eprfs-local — verified host projection

`LocalMaterializer` validates the projection shape and writes entries one by one;
each fetched handle must pass CID and byte verification before its entry is written.
`verify_projection` checks file drift only. The separate `restore_exact_tree`
checks the complete closure, then stages and promotes to a NEW destination on
Linux with no-replace semantics. It refuses external boundaries and unsupported
host semantics. Its parent must be trusted against concurrent hostile mutation.
The stage root is created private (0700). Files are synced after mode application;
directories are synced bottom-up before no-replace promotion, and the same opened
parent is synced afterward. Any required sync failure returns an error. Errors
after staging expose `ExactRestoreError::residue()` with the retained location and
whether promotion occurred. Failures and unwinding never delete that stage; process
death may leave a `.eprfs-restore-*` sibling for caller inspection. No restore runs
hooks or executables, replaces an existing checkout, or grants cleanup authority.

`capture_tree` verifies the canonical closure before and after storing it through
`DirectoryStorage`; a successful result includes synchronized object custody and
`published: false`. Both native lanes cap objects at 64 MiB and expanded closures at
1 GiB. Actual persistence depends on the filesystem/device honoring `sync_all`;
the tests inject I/O failures and process interruption, not physical power loss.
Version 0.3.3 preserves Tree v1 wire bytes and existing error-enum variants;
publication and native acceptance remain separate proofs.
